<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Texas Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Texas War of Independence · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> Revolution was the rebellion of American settlers and some Tejanos in the Mexican province of Texas against the centralist government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a>. Fighting began in October 1835; Santa Anna won at the Alamo in March 1836, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-houston">Sam Houston</a> defeated him at San Jacinto on 21 April 1836 and captured him the next day. Texas became an independent republic and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1845.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/texas-revolution.jpg" alt="Texas Revolution" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original image drawn by Joseph L. Cain; update by karanacs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campaigns_of_the_Texas_Revolution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The revolution followed a decade of political and cultural clashes between the Mexican government and the large and growing population of Anglo-American settlers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, then part of the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a> y Tejas. The Mexican government had become more centralised and had stripped Texas of its autonomy, and curbed immigration from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the holding of slaves as property. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> had abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1829 but made an exemption for Texas that was never written into the constitution. Slavery is not mentioned in the Texas Declaration of Independence, but the ownership of enslaved people mattered greatly to many settlers.</p><p>The uprising was part of a larger revolt in several provinces against the regime of President Miguel Barragán and General Santa Anna. The Mexican Congress passed the Tornel Decree, which declared that foreigners fighting Mexican troops would be treated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Fighting began on 2 October 1835 at Gonzales, over a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> the army had lent the town. On 10 October about 125 volunteers, thirty of them Tejanos, stormed the presidio at Goliad, and Texian forces laid siege to Béxar. By mid-December the small Mexican garrisons had been defeated. The Consultation, meeting on 3 November, set up a provisional government under the Constitution of 1824 but did not declare independence, and its infighting left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> with little effective government. A convention at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-on-the-brazos">Washington-on-the-Brazos</a> declared independence on 2 March 1836.</p><p>Santa Anna entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> in mid-February 1836 with his Army of Operations. His troops defeated the garrison at the Alamo on 6 March and killed almost all its defenders. General José de Urrea won on the coast and executed most of those who surrendered at Goliad. Sam Houston, now commander of the army, withdrew while terrified civilians fled in the Runaway Scrape. On 21 April his army of about 900 men surprised Santa Anna's force of about 700 at the Battle of San Jacinto and routed it. Many Mexican soldiers who tried to surrender were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Santa Anna was captured the next day. To save his life he ordered the remaining Mexican troops to retreat south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, and he signed the Treaty of Velasco, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> its independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> refused to recognise the republic, because Santa Anna had signed as a prisoner, and intermittent conflict continued into the 1840s. The Republic of Texas was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1845 as the 28th state, which led directly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Texas Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
